at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd970ca4-531c-5efa-b2d5-8a2654d8cfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571977}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd970ca4-531c-5efa-b2d5-8a2654d8cfb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571977} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d76b44d-6cd7-5750-b38b-774c366dedf2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "facb659a-1d6a-50ad-a009-4e0f9aa3e331" :question/math-academy-id "q-334183" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}\\,=$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-12}{9{(x+5)}^{4}}=\\frac{-12}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${(x+5)}^{4}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+5)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{(x+5)}^{4}}=-\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f2985fe-ea8d-5e52-b72c-0a10ddf54913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571683} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb23454-9977-5191-ad3a-c12baa1c41eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571706} {:answer/id #uuid "7405bf6b-6a0d-5b62-a398-8dece69260f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571737} {:answer/id #uuid "781f5cee-1530-50ff-9dc8-5cb5770aa8a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571745} {:answer/id #uuid "be83f313-b20b-5287-9aeb-a58682fcdc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7405bf6b-6a0d-5b62-a398-8dece69260f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571737} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5abf857-ce1f-5315-be6e-f87c73fa5383" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "126b6d2e-f9ae-5f21-89ee-ad0b88bb4a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-46965" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x-3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $3$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{x-3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e3/e3308ad7f61359df7fb4e1b0f32f1d373c703be34b353ffa100ae57c6c115e0b.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}\\frac{1}{x-3}=\\infty ,\n$$"}]}
